package gitproto

import (
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"io"
)

// MaxAdvertisementBytes caps how much of a single server-supplied
// advertisement or control message the helper buffers in memory.
//
// Every such read is an io.ReadAll into one contiguous buffer, so
// without a cap a hostile or merely broken server holds the helper's
// memory hostage just by never closing the stream — on the clone,
// fetch and push paths alike, before any authentication decision the
// caller might make about the response.
//
// The limit only ever applies to control traffic. Pack data is never
// buffered: it is streamed straight through with io.Copy (see
// handleConnect and handleStatelessConnect), so a large clone is
// unaffected by this number no matter how big the pack is. What it
// bounds is the ref advertisement, the v2 capability advertisement and
// the bundle-uri catalogue, all of which are small in practice — a v0
// ref advertisement costs roughly 50 bytes plus the ref name per ref,
// so 64 MiB is on the order of a million refs, where the largest
// public repositories sit in the tens of thousands.
const MaxAdvertisementBytes = 64 << 20

// ErrRemoteBodyTooLarge reports a server response that exceeded
// MaxAdvertisementBytes. Callers must fail on it; see ReadCapped.
var ErrRemoteBodyTooLarge = errors.New("remote response too large")

// ReadCapped reads all of r, failing with ErrRemoteBodyTooLarge once
// more than limit bytes are on offer. what names the body being read
// and appears in both error messages.
//
// Exceeding the cap is an error and never a truncation, which is the
// whole point of routing these reads through one helper. A short
// advertisement is not a smaller advertisement, it is a corrupt one:
// parsing the prefix would handnthe caller a ref list missing exactly
// the refs an attacker arranged to sit past the cap, and a truncated
// pkt-line stream can end mid-packet. Callers must therefore propagate
// the error rather than falling back to whatever was read.
func ReadCapped(r io.Reader, limit int64, what string) ([]byte, error) {
	// limit+1 so a body sitting exactly on the cap is still accepted
	// and one byte over is detectable without reading the remainder.
	buf, err := io.ReadAll(io.LimitReader(r, limit+1))
	if err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("reading %s: %w", what, err)
	}
	if int64(len(buf)) > limit {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("%w: %s exceeds %d bytes", ErrRemoteBodyTooLarge, what, limit)
	}
	return buf, nil
}
